---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Container from '../../components/common/Container';
import PageHeader from '../../components/common/PageHeader';
import ClosingBanner from '../../components/common/ClosingBanner';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/nav';

const title = 'AI Enhancer and the Neural Chip: Troubleshooting Guide | Bitperfect Player';
const description =
  'Why AI Enhancer works best with a neural chip, what happens without one, and how to set up your USB DAC for bit-perfect playback.';
---

<ContentLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    maxWidth="760px"
    backHref={withBase('/faq')}
    backLabel="Back to Knowledge Base & FAQ"
    eyebrow="Hardware & Driver Guide"
    title="Why AI Enhancer runs best on a neural chip"
    description="How AI Enhancer uses a neural chip, what runs without one, and how to set up a USB DAC."
  />

  {/* Main Centered Editorial Column */}
  <section class="py-12 lg:py-20">
    <Container>
      <article class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] space-y-14 [&_p]:max-w-[68ch]">
        {/* Section 1: The Core NPU Requirement */}
        <div id="the-real-time-neural-challenge">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            Restoring detail as music plays
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              AI Enhancer is a neural network. As a track plays, it restores high-frequency detail that MP3 and AAC remove. The result is an estimate, not the original master. It is off until you turn it on. While it works, the badge says Enhanced.
            </p>
            <p>
              AI Enhancer works on 44.1 kHz and 48 kHz PCM. It skips hi-res, DSD and cast playback. It also turns off if your phone cannot keep up, so the music does not stutter.
            </p>
            <p>
              AI Enhancer is built for a <strong>neural chip (NPU)</strong>. Phones with a supported chip run the full model on it. Other phones can run a lighter model, if their processor keeps up in real time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 2: Three Reasons AI Enhancer May Be Disabled — Clean Editorial Hairlines, Zero Nested Boxes */}
        <div id="three-reasons" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            Why AI Enhancer may be unavailable
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            First, check that Bit-perfect lock is off. It turns AI Enhancer off. If AI Enhancer still will not turn on or does not use your neural chip, check these three things:
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 border-b border-border-subtle">
            {/* Reason 01 */}
            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle py-7">
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">
                  Your phone has no supported neural chip
                </h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  The full model needs a supported neural chip: Snapdragon 8 Gen 1 up to the 8 Elite family, or the Dimensity 8300, 8400, 9300 and 9400 families. Other phones run a lighter model on the main processor. If that cannot keep up in real time, AI Enhancer turns off.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            {/* Reason 02 */}
            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle py-7">
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">
                  Your chip is newer than the current models
                </h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  Each chip needs its own model. Support for a new chip may come in a later app update. Until then, AI Enhancer works as it does on phones without a supported chip. Keep Bitperfect Player updated.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            {/* Reason 03 */}
            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle py-7">
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">
                  Some manufacturers block apps from the neural chip
                </h3>
                <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  This can happen even when the phone has a neural chip. <strong>Motorola</strong> is one reported case. When the chip does not respond, AI Enhancer works as it does on phones without one.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-3 text-body-sm text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  To ask for access, post your phone model in the official Lenovo support thread:
                </p>
                <a
                  href="https://forums.lenovo.com/topic/view/16436/10046553"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  class="mt-3 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded text-small font-semibold text-gold transition-colors duration-[var(--d-ui)] hover:text-gold-light hover:underline focus-visible:focus-ring"
                >
                  <span>Ask Lenovo / Motorola for chip access</span>
                  <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                    <path d="M18 13v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"></path>
                    <polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline>
                    <line x1="10" y1="14" x2="21" y2="3"></line>
                  </svg>
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 3: USB DAC Setup & Troubleshooting — Clean 2x2 Architectural Hairline Grid */}
        <div id="usb-dac-setup" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            Set up your USB DAC
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            To use a USB DAC with an Android phone, check these four things:
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-10 gap-y-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-5">
              <span class="mono-spec text-caption font-semibold text-gold uppercase tracking-wider">
                Hardware
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2 text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">Cable and host mode</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-small text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Micro-USB phones need an OTG cable to act as the USB host. USB-C phones do this on their own with a USB-C DAC dongle or a C-to-C cable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-5">
              <span class="mono-spec text-caption font-semibold text-gold uppercase tracking-wider">
                Android
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2 text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">USB permission prompt</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-small text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Plug in the DAC and allow access when Android asks. Bitperfect Player then has sole use of the DAC. Other apps cannot play through it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-5">
              <span class="mono-spec text-caption font-semibold text-gold uppercase tracking-wider">
                Volume
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2 text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">DAC volume and app volume</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-small text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                If your DAC has its own volume control over USB, Bitperfect Player uses it. Your file keeps its full bit depth. App volume below full is not bit-perfect, and the badge shows it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-5">
              <span class="mono-spec text-caption font-semibold text-gold uppercase tracking-wider">
                Badge
              </span>
              <h3 class="mt-2 text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary">Bit-perfect status</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-small text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Check the signal badge on Now Playing. It says Bit-perfect only when your file reaches the DAC at its own rate and bit depth, with no effects on. DSD shows its level instead, such as DSD128. Otherwise it says Enhanced, Resampled or Lossy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 4: Bitperfect Lock & Direct Bypass */}
        <div id="bitperfect-lock-bypass" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            Bit-perfect lock and direct output
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              <strong>Why direct output matters:</strong> On many phones, Android's mixer converts all audio to 48 kHz, often at 16-bit, before your DAC gets it. Direct USB output skips the mixer.
            </p>
            <p>
              Bitperfect Player has its own USB driver for UAC1 and UAC2 DACs. With no effects on and volume set on the DAC or at full in the app, your file reaches the DAC unchanged, at its own rate and bit depth, if your DAC supports them.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>What Bit-perfect lock does:</strong> It turns off every effect, including EQ, crossfeed, dither, the true-peak limiter, ReplayGain, upsampling and AI Enhancer. Volume goes to your DAC's control, or stays at full if it has none. It cannot stop a rate change your DAC needs, so check the badge.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Section 5: Battery Optimization */}
        <div id="battery-optimization" class="border-t border-border-subtle pt-12">
          <h2 class="text-title-lg font-bold text-text-primary text-balance">
            Battery use and background playback
          </h2>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-body text-pretty text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Many USB DAC dongles take power from your phone, so your battery drains faster while you listen.
            </p>
            <h3 class="text-body-lg font-bold text-text-primary pt-2">
              Keep playback going with the screen off
            </h3>
            <p>
              Many Android phones close background apps. This can stop playback when the screen turns off. To keep playback going:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-small pl-2">
              <li>Open Android <strong>Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Bitperfect Player &gt; Battery</strong>.</li>
              <li>Change the setting from <em>Optimized</em> to <strong>Unrestricted</strong>.</li>
              <li>If your phone offers it, long-press the Bitperfect Player card in the app switcher and select <strong>Lock App</strong>.</li>
              <li>In Bitperfect Player settings, set <strong>Source read-ahead</strong> to <strong>Whole file</strong>. Each track then loads into memory, so storage stays idle.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <ClosingBanner
    maxWidth="760px"
    eyebrow="Questions?"
    title="Not sure what your phone can do?"
    description="Send your phone model and DAC. We will tell you what works and what does not."
    primaryHref="mailto:support@bit-perfect.com"
    primaryLabel="Email your phone and DAC details"
    secondaryHref={withBase('/faq')}
    secondaryLabel="Read the full FAQ"
  />
</ContentLayout>
